1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,710
Hi everyone,

2
00:00:01,730 --> 00:00:08,488
and welcome back to Sewing Machine Design and Manufacturing at WenQuest.

3
00:00:08,888 --> 00:00:13,436
This is Lecture 24, making needles and rotary hooks.

4
00:00:13,836 --> 00:00:18,178
A needle pierces the fabric more than eighty times a second,

5
00:00:18,198 --> 00:00:21,449
and the hook's fits are only tens of microns.

6
00:00:21,469 --> 00:00:25,883
Today we see how these two tiny parts are made and inspected,

7
00:00:25,903 --> 00:00:30,826
and how to fix the old problem of noisy hooks, cheaply and reliably.

8
00:00:32,346 --> 00:00:35,366
Noisy hooks are a common repair call.

9
00:00:35,766 --> 00:00:41,954
If the clearance between the hook race and the basket rib is too large,

10
00:00:41,974 --> 00:00:47,026
the basket rattles; too small, and it heats up and sticks.

11
00:00:47,426 --> 00:00:51,886
A hook maker requires a diametral clearance of 10 to 30 microns.

12
00:00:52,286 --> 00:01:01,038
The race bore and the rib are ground after hardening to an economical plus or

13
00:01:01,058 --> 00:01:03,545
minus 15 microns each,

14
00:01:03,565 --> 00:01:05,026
a sigma of 5.

15
00:01:05,426 --> 00:01:08,893
Halving sigma doubles the machining cost.

16
00:01:09,293 --> 00:01:15,290
How do we reach a 99 percent yield, cheaply and reliably?

17
00:01:15,690 --> 00:01:17,270
We'll answer at the end.

18
00:01:18,790 --> 00:01:22,490
Needles: anatomy, stiffness and process.

19
00:01:24,010 --> 00:01:31,962
The size is the blade diameter: Nm 90 means 0.90 millimetre.

20
00:01:32,362 --> 00:01:33,882
The blade is a slender rod.

21
00:01:34,282 --> 00:01:39,781
Bending stiffness goes with the section's second moment of area,

22
00:01:39,801 --> 00:01:45,645
and so does the buckling load, which also falls with length squared.

23
00:01:46,045 --> 00:01:50,394
The second moment goes with diameter to the fourth power,

24
00:01:50,414 --> 00:01:52,463
so size matters enormously.

25
00:01:52,863 --> 00:02:01,565
At the same length, Nm 110 is 2.23 times as stiff as Nm 90,

26
00:02:01,585 --> 00:02:05,409
and Nm 70 only 0.37 times.

27
00:02:05,809 --> 00:02:09,830
That's one reason thick fabric needs a thick needle:

28
00:02:09,850 --> 00:02:14,337
a thin one gets pushed aside and runs into the hook point.

29
00:02:14,737 --> 00:02:17,934
It's also why straightness is critical:

30
00:02:17,954 --> 00:02:22,553
a needle that starts bent deflects even more under load.

31
00:02:24,073 --> 00:02:29,013
Needle making has five stages: stock, forming, heat treatment,

32
00:02:29,033 --> 00:02:32,693
surface treatment, and inspection and packing.

33
00:02:33,093 --> 00:02:39,009
The raw material is high-carbon wire, checked on arrival for diameter,

34
00:02:39,029 --> 00:02:44,607
cracks and decarburisation, then straightened and cut into blanks.

35
00:02:45,007 --> 00:02:49,433
The blade is reduced from shank diameter to needle size,

36
00:02:49,453 --> 00:02:54,514
about a 69 percent reduction in area, usually by rotary swaging:

37
00:02:54,534 --> 00:02:58,484
dies spin around the blank and hammer it radially,

38
00:02:58,504 --> 00:03:00,787
keeping the grain continuous.

39
00:03:01,187 --> 00:03:04,687
Uneven reduction bends the blade.

40
00:03:05,087 --> 00:03:09,099
Then the grooves and scarf are cold-pressed;

41
00:03:09,119 --> 00:03:16,064
the dies are managed by stroke count, because as they wear the scarf drifts.

42
00:03:17,584 --> 00:03:23,572
Flattening and punching the eye is the most critical operation in needle making,

43
00:03:23,592 --> 00:03:26,276
and the one most likely to go wrong.

44
00:03:26,676 --> 00:03:32,375
Grooves are pressed in from both sides to thin the eye region,

45
00:03:32,395 --> 00:03:35,696
then a very fine punch goes through.

46
00:03:36,096 --> 00:03:41,531
The punch is finer than the eye, so it wears and breaks quickly.

47
00:03:41,931 --> 00:03:46,730
As it wears, the burrs grow and the eye width drifts;

48
00:03:46,750 --> 00:03:49,366
if it is slightly off centre,

49
00:03:49,386 --> 00:03:54,911
one wall is too thin and the needle breaks at the eye in use.

50
00:03:55,311 --> 00:03:59,501
Burrs are the main cause of cut and broken thread,

51
00:03:59,521 --> 00:04:02,531
and polishing can't remove big ones.

52
00:04:02,931 --> 00:04:08,302
So this operation needs an in-line control chart of eye width,

53
00:04:08,322 --> 00:04:11,431
and punches changed by stroke count.

54
00:04:12,951 --> 00:04:19,131
After forming, the needle is still soft and must be hardened.

55
00:04:19,531 --> 00:04:20,533
Being thin,

56
00:04:20,553 --> 00:04:27,684
needles are usually heated continuously in a protective atmosphere to prevent

57
00:04:27,704 --> 00:04:30,285
oxidation and decarburising,

58
00:04:30,305 --> 00:04:31,956
then oil-quenched.

59
00:04:32,356 --> 00:04:38,558
Quenched, they are hard and brittle, so they must be tempered.

60
00:04:38,958 --> 00:04:44,321
Lower temperature: harder and more wear-resistant, but more brittle.

61
00:04:44,721 --> 00:04:47,986
Higher: tougher, bending rather than breaking,

62
00:04:48,006 --> 00:04:50,414
but the point and eye wear faster.

63
00:04:50,814 --> 00:04:55,594
The exact value is a needle maker's core process data.

64
00:04:55,994 --> 00:05:02,866
Slender needles always distort when hardened, so each one is straightened,

65
00:05:02,886 --> 00:05:08,361
then polished to remove burrs and nickel- or chrome-plated.

66
00:05:08,761 --> 00:05:13,179
Finally machine vision checks the shape of every needle;

67
00:05:13,199 --> 00:05:17,141
hardness and plating can only be sampled by batch.

68
00:05:18,661 --> 00:05:21,961
Inspection and statistical process control.

69
00:05:23,481 --> 00:05:28,913
What the factory really wants to know is whether this operation can reliably

70
00:05:28,933 --> 00:05:30,061
make good parts.

71
00:05:30,461 --> 00:05:35,788
Cp only compares the tolerance band with the process spread;

72
00:05:35,808 --> 00:05:39,798
Cpk also asks how far the mean is off centre.

73
00:05:40,198 --> 00:05:47,967
A common target is 1.33, the nearer limit four sigma from the mean.

74
00:05:48,367 --> 00:05:53,692
The example: eye width 0.360 plus or minus 0.020,

75
00:05:53,712 --> 00:06:00,236
five needles per subgroup from in-line vision, 25 subgroups,

76
00:06:00,256 --> 00:06:04,490
sigma estimated from the average range.

77
00:06:04,890 --> 00:06:13,870
Cp comes out about 1.70 and Cpk about 1.52: capable.

78
00:06:14,270 --> 00:06:20,953
But once the punch wears and the mean moves up one sigma,

79
00:06:20,973 --> 00:06:27,891
Cpk falls to 1.17 and the reject rate rises about 68 times.

80
00:06:29,411 --> 00:06:32,819
How do we catch a drift early?

81
00:06:33,219 --> 00:06:36,176
With a control chart.

82
00:06:36,576 --> 00:06:42,713
Plot each subgroup's mean in time order; the centre line is the grand mean,

83
00:06:42,733 --> 00:06:49,116
and the limits are the grand mean plus or minus A-two times the average range.

84
00:06:49,516 --> 00:06:55,150
Note that the limits come from the process's own spread and have nothing to

85
00:06:55,170 --> 00:06:57,110
do with the specification.

86
00:06:57,510 --> 00:07:03,486
A point outside means the process has changed, so stop and look for the cause,

87
00:07:03,506 --> 00:07:06,253
even if the needle is still in spec.

88
00:07:06,653 --> 00:07:10,901
Here a one-sigma drift starts at subgroup 26,

89
00:07:10,921 --> 00:07:17,731
and with three per subgroup it takes around ten subgroups before a point

90
00:07:17,751 --> 00:07:19,913
breaks the upper limit.

91
00:07:21,433 --> 00:07:25,715
How fast it alarms can be worked out in advance:

92
00:07:25,735 --> 00:07:31,092
the probability that a subgroup falls outside after a drift,

93
00:07:31,112 --> 00:07:35,573
and its reciprocal is the average run length, ARL.

94
00:07:35,973 --> 00:07:44,246
With no drift there's one false alarm every 370 subgroups on average.

95
00:07:44,646 --> 00:07:46,908
After a one-sigma drift,

96
00:07:46,928 --> 00:07:52,329
subgroups of three take nearly ten subgroups to catch it;

97
00:07:52,349 --> 00:07:58,226
a half-sigma drift takes about 33 even with five per subgroup.

98
00:07:58,626 --> 00:08:04,167
The bigger the subgroup, the less the mean scatters and the sooner the alarm,

99
00:08:04,187 --> 00:08:06,406
at the price of measuring more.

100
00:08:06,806 --> 00:08:10,405
With full inspection you can take big subgroups;

101
00:08:10,425 --> 00:08:14,703
destructive checks like hardness must rely on prevention.

102
00:08:16,223 --> 00:08:19,440
Hooks: grinding and selective assembly.

103
00:08:20,960 --> 00:08:25,813
The basket's rib slides in the race inside the hook body.

104
00:08:26,213 --> 00:08:30,770
Last lecture's hook gap had an adjustable link;

105
00:08:30,790 --> 00:08:34,470
the race-to-basket clearance has none.

106
00:08:34,870 --> 00:08:37,589
It depends only on two diameters:

107
00:08:37,609 --> 00:08:42,901
the nominal clearance plus the difference of the two deviations,

108
00:08:42,921 --> 00:08:46,552
with only plus or minus 10 microns to spare.

109
00:08:46,952 --> 00:08:53,558
If any body fits any basket, the clearance's sigma is the root sum of squares.

110
00:08:53,958 --> 00:09:00,752
Both parts at a sigma of 5, already decent economical accuracy,

111
00:09:00,772 --> 00:09:08,538
give a clearance sigma of 7.07 microns and only about 84.5 percent good.

112
00:09:08,938 --> 00:09:15,707
For 99 percent each part must be under 2.7 microns, nearly half,

113
00:09:15,727 --> 00:09:18,358
at about double the cost.

114
00:09:18,758 --> 00:09:22,978
The chart alternates between the two accuracies.

115
00:09:24,498 --> 00:09:27,078
Grouped matching takes another approach.

116
00:09:27,478 --> 00:09:33,392
The parts are still made to economical accuracy, but every one is measured;

117
00:09:33,412 --> 00:09:38,694
the range of plus or minus 15 microns is split into k equal groups,

118
00:09:38,714 --> 00:09:41,938
and parts are paired only within a group.

119
00:09:42,338 --> 00:09:49,638
Two deviations in the same group of width w differ by less than w.

120
00:09:50,038 --> 00:09:56,730
With three groups w is 10 microns, so the clearance is always within 10 to 30,

121
00:09:56,750 --> 00:09:59,570
whatever the parts' own accuracy.

122
00:09:59,970 --> 00:10:02,747
That's measurement instead of accuracy.

123
00:10:03,147 --> 00:10:08,496
It has three costs: measuring everything and storing by group;

124
00:10:08,516 --> 00:10:14,557
gauge error misgrading parts near the boundaries; and, most important,

125
00:10:14,577 --> 00:10:18,800
both parts must have equal counts in every group.

126
00:10:20,320 --> 00:10:26,921
What the shop floor most often overlooks is that the two parts' distributions

127
00:10:26,941 --> 00:10:27,867
must match.

128
00:10:28,267 --> 00:10:32,102
This shows the count in each of the three groups,

129
00:10:32,122 --> 00:10:35,564
blue for hook bodies and orange for baskets.

130
00:10:35,964 --> 00:10:42,781
After the race grinder's wheel is dressed, its mean shifts by 4 microns.

131
00:10:43,181 --> 00:10:49,694
Pairs within a group still have good clearance, so the yield barely moves,

132
00:10:49,714 --> 00:10:53,843
but the blue and orange bars no longer line up,

133
00:10:53,863 --> 00:11:00,641
and leftovers rise from 1.7 percent to about 27, pushing up the cost per set.

134
00:11:01,041 --> 00:11:06,501
So the matching station must watch not just the yield but the count in each group.

135
00:11:08,021 --> 00:11:12,161
These ideas show up all over robotics.

136
00:11:12,561 --> 00:11:17,828
A joint's precision gearbox must hold backlash to arc-minutes;

137
00:11:17,848 --> 00:11:21,409
making every part that accurate is costly,

138
00:11:21,429 --> 00:11:28,231
so pins and bearings are often graded by measured size and fitted within groups,

139
00:11:28,251 --> 00:11:34,541
just like races and baskets, and again both distributions must be watched.

140
00:11:34,941 --> 00:11:42,127
The needle factory's in-line vision inspection is itself a classic

141
00:11:42,147 --> 00:11:44,093
robot-vision task.

142
00:11:44,493 --> 00:11:49,350
Control-chart thinking carries over to robot maintenance too:

143
00:11:49,370 --> 00:11:53,588
chart joint-motor current and vibration in subgroups,

144
00:11:53,608 --> 00:11:56,627
alarm when the mean leaves its limits,

145
00:11:56,647 --> 00:12:00,145
and service the gearbox before it wears out.

146
00:12:01,665 --> 00:12:04,635
There's selective assembly in daily life too.

147
00:12:05,035 --> 00:12:09,844
A shoe shop matches shoes to feet by size group.

148
00:12:10,244 --> 00:12:16,412
The finer the sizes, the better the fit, but every size has to be stocked,

149
00:12:16,432 --> 00:12:18,670
and the slow sizes pile up.

150
00:12:19,070 --> 00:12:23,416
That's the trade-off between the number of groups and the leftovers:

151
00:12:23,436 --> 00:12:25,600
finer groups match more precisely,

152
00:12:25,620 --> 00:12:28,810
but bring more leftovers and more management cost.

153
00:12:30,330 --> 00:12:32,188
Back to the opening problem.

154
00:12:32,588 --> 00:12:43,479
Fully interchangeable: about 84.5 percent good at about 24.9 yuan a set.

155
00:12:43,879 --> 00:12:48,370
No grouping, just tighter accuracy,

156
00:12:48,390 --> 00:12:58,294
each sigma at 2.6 microns to leave a margin: 99.2 percent at about 39.8 yuan.

157
00:12:58,694 --> 00:13:04,870
Three groups over plus or minus 15 microns, 10 microns wide,

158
00:13:04,890 --> 00:13:11,892
with 1 micron gauge error: 99.6 percent good, 1.7 percent leftovers,

159
00:13:11,912 --> 00:13:19,018
about 22.3 yuan a set, some 44 percent cheaper than tighter accuracy.

160
00:13:19,418 --> 00:13:22,279
This holds only if the two distributions match,

161
00:13:22,299 --> 00:13:25,958
so the matching station must keep watching the group counts.

162
00:13:27,478 --> 00:13:31,663
Now Virtual lab 24-1, hook matching and process capability,

163
00:13:31,683 --> 00:13:36,580
which you can do yourself on WenQuest. Results and tasks are blurred.

164
00:13:36,600 --> 00:13:41,997
On page one, the left shows the clearance distribution and the group counts;

165
00:13:42,017 --> 00:13:44,990
on the right you set the number of groups,

166
00:13:45,010 --> 00:13:47,698
the part accuracy and the gauge error.

167
00:13:48,098 --> 00:13:52,518
Task one: no groups. Turn down both parts' sigma,

168
00:13:52,538 --> 00:13:59,495
watch the distribution narrow, check for yourself the accuracy we worked out,

169
00:13:59,515 --> 00:14:02,485
and watch the cost per set climb.

170
00:14:02,885 --> 00:14:10,300
Task two: put the accuracy back at 5 microns. Try two groups first, then three,

171
00:14:10,320 --> 00:14:15,570
four and five in turn, record yield, leftovers and cost,

172
00:14:15,590 --> 00:14:20,182
and explain why you choose your number of groups.

173
00:14:20,582 --> 00:14:25,335
Then press Inject: race grinding mean plus 4 microns,

174
00:14:25,355 --> 00:14:32,269
and watch the blue and orange bars in the group chart move apart. The remedy,

175
00:14:32,289 --> 00:14:36,682
which side to move and by how much, is up to you.

176
00:14:37,082 --> 00:14:41,016
Task three is on page two, the eye-width control chart.

177
00:14:41,036 --> 00:14:45,545
With three needles per subgroup and a one-sigma drift injected,

178
00:14:45,565 --> 00:14:47,414
see which subgroup alarms;

179
00:14:47,434 --> 00:14:52,734
then choose the subgroup size so the average run length is no more than 5.

180
00:14:52,754 --> 00:14:56,329
The guide and report template are on the platform.

181
00:14:57,849 --> 00:15:02,650
One wheel dressing at the hook maker can reach all the way to the machine

182
00:15:02,670 --> 00:15:03,641
assembly plant.

183
00:15:03,661 --> 00:15:06,085
This is the WenQuest digital factory.

184
00:15:06,105 --> 00:15:09,849
The assembly line measures the hook gap on every machine,

185
00:15:09,869 --> 00:15:13,152
the readings go over the bus into a control chart,

186
00:15:13,172 --> 00:15:18,171
and after one batch of hooks goes in the data starts to drift and the system

187
00:15:18,191 --> 00:15:19,228
raises an alarm.

188
00:15:19,628 --> 00:15:24,838
Switch to the trace page and follow the chain backwards: the alarmed machine,

189
00:15:24,858 --> 00:15:29,525
the hook batch it carries, the race grinder that batch was ground on,

190
00:15:29,545 --> 00:15:34,756
and the clue: the wheel-dressing interval was stretched from 50 parts to 150.

191
00:15:34,776 --> 00:15:38,288
That's exactly the mean drift we talked about today.

192
00:15:38,688 --> 00:15:43,370
Drag the serial number and look at the machine records one by one.

193
00:15:43,390 --> 00:15:48,855
Because measurements reach the history database with lot and machine numbers,

194
00:15:48,875 --> 00:15:54,697
a problem can be traced from the finished machine all the way back to the grinder.

195
00:15:54,717 --> 00:15:58,900
We'll walk through the whole digital factory in Lecture 27.

196
00:16:00,420 --> 00:16:04,578
Your assignment has four questions. One:

197
00:16:04,598 --> 00:16:10,741
compare the blade stiffness of Nm 120 and Nm 65 with Nm 90,

198
00:16:10,761 --> 00:16:18,470
then find the one-sided Cpk for straightness, before and after punch wear.

199
00:16:18,870 --> 00:16:25,469
Two: for a 0.8-sigma drift to alarm within 6 subgroups on average,

200
00:16:25,489 --> 00:16:32,891
what is the smallest subgroup size? Check one case on page two of the lab.

201
00:16:33,291 --> 00:16:37,619
Three: use Lab 24-1 to propose a hook-matching plan,

202
00:16:37,639 --> 00:16:40,964
then handle a 4 micron race shift. Four:

203
00:16:40,984 --> 00:16:45,228
write an inspection item and a measurement message,

204
00:16:45,248 --> 00:16:51,751
and work out the capacity with one more race grinder, and the next bottleneck.

205
00:16:52,151 --> 00:16:57,651
Submit the assignment and the lab report on the WenQuest platform.

206
00:16:59,170 --> 00:17:04,320
There's also a ten-question quiz. For example,

207
00:17:04,340 --> 00:17:10,950
the eye-width spec is 0.360 plus or minus 0.020 millimetre,

208
00:17:10,970 --> 00:17:18,030
with a process mean of 0.358 and a sigma of 0.005. What is Cpk?

209
00:17:18,430 --> 00:17:19,899
Or true or false:

210
00:17:19,919 --> 00:17:27,081
the control limits of an X-bar chart are worked out from the specification limits,

211
00:17:27,101 --> 00:17:31,810
so a point inside them means the eye width is in spec.

212
00:17:32,210 --> 00:17:37,670
You get three attempts, and the explanations appear as soon as you submit.

213
00:17:39,190 --> 00:17:40,650
Three takeaways.

214
00:17:41,050 --> 00:17:46,782
One: needles and hooks stand for two ways of manufacturing.

215
00:17:47,182 --> 00:17:53,046
Needles rely on consistent forming and full vision inspection,

216
00:17:53,066 --> 00:17:59,689
with eye punching and tempering most critical; hooks rely on grinding,

217
00:17:59,709 --> 00:18:01,682
lapping and matching.

218
00:18:02,082 --> 00:18:06,593
Two: Cpk tells you whether a process is capable,

219
00:18:06,613 --> 00:18:10,274
a control chart whether it has changed,

220
00:18:10,294 --> 00:18:14,144
and bigger subgroups catch drifts sooner.

221
00:18:14,544 --> 00:18:15,061
Three:

222
00:18:15,081 --> 00:18:21,950
grouped matching swaps accuracy for measurement and reaches a high yield with

223
00:18:21,970 --> 00:18:23,471
economical parts,

224
00:18:23,491 --> 00:18:28,750
but the two distributions must match, or leftovers pile up.

225
00:18:29,150 --> 00:18:31,617
The slides, the virtual lab,

226
00:18:31,637 --> 00:18:37,836
the assignment and the quiz are all on the WenQuest learning platform,

227
00:18:37,856 --> 00:18:43,788
at learn.wenquestrobotics.com. Sign up for free and start learning.

228
00:18:44,188 --> 00:18:45,088
